{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"680296ff8a4593553a9eff5129d4f195e4d96682338356321b2652595c8fe1b8","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_8941d0ff5081a4d78435a926e4852a54c2ae4f98f6786323b4ff53880d2e6af5","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound extension of the Learning to Optimize framework, specifically addressing the high-dimensional challenges of training neural networks through a structured decomposition of the policy search problem. The methodological approach is well-motivated and mathematically consistent, effectively leveraging permutation invariance to reduce computational complexity. Experimental results demonstrate strong generalization capabilities across diverse datasets and architectures, validating the core claims. However, the evaluation relies on single-run visualizations without statistical significance testing, and the reproducibility is limited by the absence of code and specific hyperparameter details for the meta-training process.","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_identity_sha256":"d04cf0dc89643606f25a31d5d3c6f5c56ad57147a6523e8629a29d22dfb36f9c","prompt_identity_sha256":"9e255031f71d854fb25df900819ae680110b4641a85afb086cb7cc48f294cdca","raw_response_sha256":"4d4d4ab9313b18c0b9dba71858b9e99d0e71f7a359dbb6008adcd8f534b6a6d8","request_identity_sha256":"19fa5e423e6f561c51c52ba3b3e49e514d46e88dddad957a3576b42258d4a111","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"6a550b1f09993ce9bfdd2db2c653b409a1eb1f2a0435327cac57b08548a3e881","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-026","SRC-028","SRC-030"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and well-formulated. The authors correctly identify the computational bottleneck of Guided Policy Search (GPS) in high-dimensional spaces, where the Linear Quadratic Gaussian (LQG) solver scales cubically with state dimensionality. The introduction of permutation invariance as a structural prior is a mathematically consistent solution that decomposes the optimization problem into independent subproblems for each coordinate group (weight matrices and bias vectors). This decomposition is logically derived from the symmetry of the neural network architecture and ensures that the learned policy respects the underlying structure of the optimization problem. The formulation of the state and observation features, which rely on summary statistics of recent iterates and gradients, is appropriate for handling the stochasticity inherent in neural network training. No major conceptual or implementation errors are apparent in the derivation of the constrained optimization problem or the application of the block-diagonal structure to the transition and cost models.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-034","SRC-036","SRC-037","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The experimental design is adequate for validating the primary claims of generalization and robustness. The authors meta-train on a single objective function (MNIST) and evaluate on three distinct datasets (TFD, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100) with varying characteristics, which effectively tests the task-independence of the learned optimizer. The comparison includes seven standard hand-engineered algorithms (SGD, Momentum, Conjugate Gradient, L-BFGS, ADAM, AdaGrad, RMSprop) and a learned baseline from prior work (Andrychowicz et al., 2016), providing a comprehensive set of baselines. The experiments vary the neural network architecture (48 vs. 100/200 units) and the mini-batch size (64 vs. 10) to assess robustness to model size and gradient noise. The results, presented in Figures 1-5, consistently show the proposed method outperforming baselines in terms of objective value convergence. However, the evaluation is limited to a single run per configuration, and the reliance on visual inspection of learning curves rather than quantitative metrics (e.g., final loss, time to convergence) limits the precision of the performance comparison.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-034","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient high-level details to understand the method, including the architecture of the meta-learner (single layer of 128 LSTM cells), the time horizon (400 iterations), and the mini-batch size (64) used during meta-training. The feature definitions for state and observation are explicitly described, which is crucial for replicating the input processing. However, the paper lacks specific hyperparameters for the meta-training process itself, such as the learning rate for the LSTM, the regularization coefficients, or the specific tuning procedure for the hand-engineered baselines. The statement that 'all algorithms are tuned on the training objective function' is vague regarding the search space and method used for this tuning. Additionally, there is no mention of code availability or a repository, which hinders direct reproduction of the results. The absence of these implementation details makes it difficult for other researchers to verify the exact conditions under which the reported performance was achieved.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-006","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The paper positions itself clearly within the context of learning optimization algorithms, distinguishing between the task-independent approach of Li & Malik (2016) and the task-dependent approach of Andrychowicz et al. (2016). The related work section provides a thorough categorization of meta-learning methods, effectively situating the proposed work in the 'learning how to learn' category. The comparison against prior work is appropriate, as the authors directly compare their method against the supervised learning baseline from Andrychowicz et al. (2016) under identical meta-training conditions. The inclusion of standard hand-engineered optimizers as baselines is standard practice and provides a meaningful reference point for the utility of the learned algorithm. The literature review is comprehensive and covers relevant recent work in hyperparameter optimization and meta-learning, ensuring that the contribution is well-contextualized within the broader field.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-037","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor of the evaluation is limited. The paper presents results as single-run learning curves (Figures 1-5) without reporting error bars, confidence intervals, or standard deviations across multiple random seeds. Given the stochastic nature of both the meta-training process (due to stochastic gradients) and the base-level training, the variance in performance can be significant. The claim that the proposed method 'consistently outperforms' other algorithms is based on visual inspection of these single runs, which does not provide statistical evidence of significance. Without multiple runs and appropriate statistical tests, it is not possible to determine whether the observed improvements are robust or due to chance. The lack of quantitative summary statistics (e.g., mean and standard deviation of final loss) further limits the ability to assess the reliability of the reported results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"8f855d55401be6e09a03aa5f6dd531be8f5ee2d72ec6b88906bf230186341f25","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_ordinal":97,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"4d4d4ab9313b18c0b9dba71858b9e99d0e71f7a359dbb6008adcd8f534b6a6d8","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"a7e01848001fad7f47bdc908712e31469750fcc61612dfda987012579b59fdd4","request_identity_sha256":"19fa5e423e6f561c51c52ba3b3e49e514d46e88dddad957a3576b42258d4a111","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1447,"prompt_tokens":18508,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19955},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"19fa5e423e6f561c51c52ba3b3e49e514d46e88dddad957a3576b42258d4a111","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}